{"schemaVersion":1,"systemCode":"BGN-PCGN:udm-Cyrl:Latn:2011","dependencies":[],"metadata":{"authority_id":"bgnpcgn","id":"2011","language":"iso-639-2:udm","source_script":"Cyrl","destination_script":"Latn","name":"Romanization of Udmurt (2011)","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/permanent-committee-on-geographical-names","creation_date":"2011","confirmation_date":"2017-11","description":"The BGN/PCGN system for Udmurt is designed for use in romanizing names written in the\n    Udmurt Cyrillic alphabet. Udmurt (sometimes known as Votyak) is a member of the Permian\n    branch of the Finno-Ugric languages and is an official language alongside Russian in the\n    Russian Federation's republic of Udmurtiya; it has existed in written form since the late\n    18th century, and first appeared in its present Cyrillic orthography in 1905.","notes":["Е should be romanized ye word-initially, after a vowel, after Й, Ъ, Ь, and after the palatalizing consonants Д, З, Л, Н, С and Т. It should be romanized e elsewhere.","In order to retain reversibility, where two consonant characters appear together and the resulting romanization is equivalent to a Roman-script digraph occurring in the system (i.e. gh, zh, tl, sh or ts), a medial dot may be employed between the Roman-script consonants in order to distinguish these from the Roman-script digraphs.","The character ё is always romanized yo, independently of position.","An inventory of letter-diacritic combinations, with their Unicode encoding, in addition to the unmodified letters of the basic Roman script is:","All apostrophes appearing in romanization are U+2019","Ï (U+00CF)  ï (U+00EF)  Ö (U+00D6)  ö (U+00F6)","The Romanization columns show only lowercase forms but, when romanizing, uppercase and lowercase Roman letters as appropriate should be used."]},"stages":[{"kind":"stage","name":"main","rules":[{"kind":"sub","from":{"kind":"group","items":[{"kind":"alias","name":"boundary"},{"kind":"string","value":"Е"}]},"t